We’ll create a standalone, fast CLI that **does not need Puppeteer** at runtime. It will reuse the proof‑of‑work WASM (`deepseek.wasm`) and the same API endpoints that `deepterm` uses, plus your existing features (session management, local history). First, you need to extract your token once from the browser session you already have.

---

## Step 1 – Extract the token from your authenticated Puppeteer profile

The token was not in cookies; it’s stored in `localStorage`. Run this one‑time script **from your `~/deepseek-cli` directory** (where your `browser-data` folder is):

```javascript
// get_token_once.js
import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';

const CHROMIUM_PATH = '/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/chromium-browser';

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
    headless: 'new',
    executablePath: CHROMIUM_PATH,
    userDataDir: './browser-data',
    args: ['--no-sandbox', '--disable-setuid-sandbox', '--disable-dev-shm-usage', '--disable-gpu'],
  });
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://chat.deepseek.com/', { waitUntil: 'networkidle2', timeout: 30000 });

  // Intercept a request to grab the Authorization header
  let token = null;
  page.on('request', req => {
    if (req.url().includes('/api/v0/users/current')) {
      token = req.headers()['authorization'];
    }
  });

  // Wait a bit for the request to fire
  await page.waitForTimeout(3000);

  if (token) {
    console.log(token);
  } else {
    // Fallback: try localStorage keys
    token = await page.evaluate(() => {
      return localStorage.getItem('chat_token') ||
             localStorage.getItem('userToken') ||
             '';
    });
    console.log(token || '❌ Could not find token.');
  }

  await browser.close();
})();
```

